# C#/.NET coding style

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic style rules to modern C#
idiom (new project templates enable nullable reference types by
default since .NET 6/C# 10 — the language itself still defaults
annotations/warnings off, so an existing project may need `<Nullable>
enable</Nullable>` added explicitly; primary constructors and
collection expressions since C# 12/.NET 8).
Applies only to `*.cs` files — everything not C#-specific still comes
from the common rules this file `extends`.

## Naming and structure

- `PascalCase` for types, methods, properties, and public/protected
  members; `camelCase` for locals and parameters; a private field is
  `_camelCase` with a leading underscore, not `m_field` or bare
  `camelCase` that can collide with a parameter name.
- Interfaces are prefixed `I` (`IOrderRepository`), never suffixed
  (`OrderRepositoryInterface`).
- One public type per file, file named after the type
  (`OrderService.cs` for `class OrderService`), unless the type is a
  small private nested/support type used only inside its owner.
- File-scoped namespaces (`namespace Orders;`) instead of a nested
  block, unless the project's existing files already use block-scoped
  namespaces — match what is there.

## Records, classes, and immutability

- Prefer a `record`/`record class` for a type whose identity is its
  value (a DTO, a command, an event, an immutable snapshot) — it gets
  structural equality and a `with` expression for free. Reach for `class`
  when the type has identity beyond its data, mutable state that changes
  over its lifetime, or entity semantics an ORM tracks by reference/key.
- Use `record struct` for a small, frequently-allocated value type where
  avoiding heap allocation matters; default to a reference `record`
  otherwise.
- Favor `init`-only properties (or a primary constructor) over a
  public setter on anything that should not change after construction —
  a mutable public setter on a "value" type invites accidental aliasing
  bugs.

## Pattern matching and modern idiom

- Prefer pattern matching (`is`, `switch` expressions, property patterns,
  list patterns) over a chain of `if`/`else if` type checks or casts —
  `if (shape is Circle { Radius: > 0 } c)` reads its own intent; a cast
  followed by a separate null/type check does not.
- Use a primary constructor (`class OrderService(IOrderRepository repo)`)
  for a type whose constructor only assigns its parameters to fields —
  it removes the boilerplate field-and-assignment block. Fall back to an
  explicit constructor body once it needs validation, logic, or more than
  trivial assignment.
- Use collection expressions (`int[] xs = [1, 2, 3];`, `List<T> ys = [];`)
  for new collection-literal code instead of `new[] { ... }`/
  `new List<T> { ... }`, unless the project's existing style has not
  adopted them yet — match what is there rather than mixing both styles
  in one file.
- Expression-bodied members (`public int Total => Items.Sum(i => i.Price);`)
  for a single-expression method/property/operator; keep a full block
  body once the member needs more than one statement or local reasoning
  step — do not force multi-statement logic into one expression via `;`
  chaining or nested ternaries.

## Nullable reference types

- New projects have the nullable annotation and warning contexts on by
  default (`<Nullable>enable</Nullable>` in the project file) — keep it
  on; do not add `<Nullable>disable</Nullable>` or a blanket
  `#nullable disable` to make warnings go away.
- A parameter, property, or return type that can genuinely be absent is
  annotated `?` (`string? middleName`); a public member with no `?` is a
  promise to callers that it is never null — do not violate that promise
  with a hidden `null` return "just this once."
- Use `ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(value)` at a public API's entry
  point for a reference-typed parameter that must not be null, instead of
  a hand-rolled `if (value is null) throw new ArgumentNullException(...)`.
- Resolve a nullability warning by fixing the actual null path (a real
  null check, a non-null initializer, restructuring so the compiler can
  see the invariant) — never with the null-forgiving operator (`value!`)
  as a substitute for an actual guard, except at a boundary where you can
  state in a comment exactly why the value is provably non-null there.

## `var` usage

- Use `var` when the right-hand side already makes the type obvious
  (`var order = new Order();`, `var items = order.Items.ToList();`) or
  when the concrete type is long/generic and adds no clarity
  (`var lookup = new Dictionary<string, List<OrderLine>>();`).
- Use an explicit type when `var` would hide the type at a call site that
  matters for reading correctness (`var result = Parse(input);` where
  `Parse`'s return type is not obvious from the name) or when the
  declared type differs meaningfully from the initializer's runtime type
  (assigning a derived type but declaring the base).
